# APIForge

**An internal API marketplace and governance platform.** Teams publish their APIs to one catalogue; developers discover them, check their live health and request access; owners approve consumers and manage versions; the platform team reviews and audits everything.

Built with **ASP.NET Core 10** (Clean Architecture, EF Core, SignalR) and **Angular 21** (standalone components, signals, zoneless change detection).

> Most portfolio projects are CRUD over a business domain. APIForge solves an engineering-platform problem — API sprawl — and exercises the parts of a .NET stack that make a profile look senior: background services, real-time push, token rotation, resource-level authorization, audit trails, caching and API lifecycle management.

---

## What it does

| Area | Capabilities |
|---|---|
| **Discovery** | Searchable catalogue with filters (team, domain, lifecycle, tag, minimum availability) and sorting. Filters live in the URL, so every view is shareable. Each API shows its last 24 health checks as a live strip. |
| **Lifecycle governance** | Draft → Submitted → Under review → Published → Production → Deprecated → Retired, enforced in the domain model. Review steps are reserved for platform admins. Consumers are notified on deprecation. |
| **Versioning** | Release versions as Beta or Production. Releasing to production deprecates the previous version with a configurable support window (default 180 days). Compare any two versions to see which operations were added or removed. |
| **OpenAPI import** | Upload or paste an OpenAPI 3.x / Swagger 2.0 JSON document; operations are extracted, grouped by tag and searchable. The original document can be downloaded again. |
| **Access workflow** | Developers request specific permissions for a named application and environment. Owners approve (optionally narrowing permissions and setting expiry) or reject with a reason. Low-risk APIs can auto-approve. |
| **Credentials** | Approval creates a client ID. Only the consumer can generate the client secret; it is shown **once**, stored as a SHA-256 hash, and can be rotated or revoked. |
| **Health monitoring** | A `BackgroundService` probes every published API each minute, classifies it as Healthy, Degraded or Down, and pushes results to browsers over SignalR. Owners are notified when their API goes down. |
| **Health score** | A 0–100 score combining availability, error rate, P95 latency, documentation, security and governance — with a breakdown explaining every point lost. |
| **Usage analytics** | Requests, success/error rates, average and P95 latency, and status-class breakdown over 24 hours, 7 days or 30 days. |
| **Audit log** | Every sensitive action (sign-ins, lifecycle moves, releases, approvals, secret rotation, role changes, health changes) is written in the **same transaction** as the change. |
| **Administration** | Manage roles, teams and account status. Deactivation revokes all sessions. |

---

## Tech stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Frontend | Angular 21 — standalone components, signals, zoneless, lazy-loaded routes, reactive forms, `@microsoft/signalr` |
| Backend | ASP.NET Core 10 Web API, Clean Architecture (Domain / Application / Infrastructure / API / Contracts) |
| Data | SQL Server, Entity Framework Core 10 (interceptors, primitive collections, `ExecuteUpdate/Delete`) |
| Auth | JWT access tokens (15 min) + rotating refresh tokens in an HttpOnly cookie, RBAC + permission policies + resource checks |
| Real time | SignalR (strongly typed hub) |
| Caching | `IDistributedCache` — Redis when configured, in-memory otherwise |
| Docs | Built-in OpenAPI (`Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi`) + Swagger UI |
| Delivery | Docker, docker compose, nginx, GitHub Actions |

---

## Architecture

```
                 Angular 21 SPA  ──── SignalR (/hubs/platform) ────┐
                       │ HTTPS /api                                │
                       ▼                                           │
 ┌──────────────────────── ApiForge.API ─────────────────────────┐ │
 │ Controllers · JWT auth · permission policies · rate limiting   │◄┘
 │ ProblemDetails · security headers · OpenAPI · PlatformHub      │
 └───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘
                                 ▼
 ┌──────────────────── ApiForge.Application ─────────────────────┐
 │ Use cases: catalogue, versions, access, credentials, health,   │
 │ analytics, dashboard, admin, audit · OpenAPI parser · Authz    │
 └──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┘
                ▼                                 ▼
 ┌─────── ApiForge.Domain ───────┐   ┌──── ApiForge.Infrastructure ────┐
 │ Entities & business rules:    │   │ EF Core + SQL Server, interceptor│
 │ lifecycle transitions,        │   │ PBKDF2 hashing, JWT issuing      │
 │ version release/deprecation,  │   │ Redis cache, HTTP health probe   │
 │ access decisions, credential  │   │ HealthMonitoringWorker           │
 │ rules, health score           │   │ (BackgroundService), seed data   │
 └───────────────────────────────┘   └──────────────────────────────────┘
```

Dependencies point inwards: **Domain** has no dependencies; **Application** depends on Domain and abstractions it defines (`IApplicationDbContext`, `ICurrentUser`, `IHealthProbe`, `IRealtimePublisher`…); **Infrastructure** implements them; **API** composes everything. **Contracts** holds the request/response DTOs and depends on nothing.

---

## Getting started

### Option 1 — Docker (everything in one command)

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

| URL | What |
|---|---|
| http://localhost:4200 | The web app |
| http://localhost:4200/swagger | Interactive API docs |
| http://localhost:5080 | The API directly |

SQL Server, Redis, the API and the nginx-served frontend all start together. The database is created and seeded on first run.

### Option 2 — Run locally

**Prerequisites:** .NET 10 SDK, Node.js 22.12+ (or 20.19+/24+), and SQL Server (LocalDB on Windows, or `docker run` a SQL Server container).

```bash
# 1. API — http://localhost:5080/swagger
cd backend
dotnet run --project src/ApiForge.API

# 2. Web app — http://localhost:4200 (proxies /api and /hubs to :5080)
cd frontend
npm install
npm start
```

The default connection string targets `(localdb)\MSSQLLocalDB`. To use another server, set it without touching config files:

```bash
dotnet user-secrets set "ConnectionStrings:ApiForge" "Server=localhost,1433;Database=ApiForge;User Id=sa;Password=...;TrustServerCertificate=True" --project src/ApiForge.API
```

Outside Development, `Auth:SigningKey` **must** be supplied (≥ 32 characters) via user-secrets or the `Auth__SigningKey` environment variable — the API refuses to start without it.

