## Engineering decisions worth discussing

**Session security.** Access tokens live only in memory and expire after 15 minutes. Refresh tokens are 512-bit random values in an `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Strict` cookie scoped to `/api/auth`, stored server-side only as SHA-256 hashes. Every refresh rotates the token; presenting an already-rotated token is treated as theft and revokes every session for that user. Sign-in is rate-limited per IP, and unknown emails still cost a full PBKDF2 verification so response timing cannot reveal which accounts exist.

**Authorization in three layers.** Roles map to fine-grained permissions (`api.create`, `access.approve`, `audit.read`…), and each permission is an ASP.NET Core policy. Resource-level rules then decide ownership — an API owner can only manage their own team's APIs — and lifecycle review transitions are reserved for platform admins regardless of permission.

**Client secrets.** A secret is generated only by the consumer who owns the credential, returned once with `Cache-Control: no-store`, and stored as a SHA-256 hash (secrets are 256-bit random, so a slow hash adds nothing). Revoking clears the hash immediately.

**Health monitoring on the server.** Probing runs in a `BackgroundService` with a `PeriodicTimer`, bounded parallelism and per-probe timeouts — monitoring never depends on someone having a dashboard open. Results are recorded, the 24-hour aggregates cache is invalidated, owners are notified on transitions to Down, and every result is pushed over SignalR. Probe history is pruned after a retention window.

**Versioning without breaking consumers.** Releasing a production version never removes the old one; it deprecates it with a published support-until date, notifies every active consumer and keeps the migration guide alongside. The comparison view shows exactly which operations a consumer loses.

**Audit logging.** Audit entries are added to the same `DbContext` unit of work as the change, so a change and its audit record commit or fail together. Timestamps are stamped by an EF Core `SaveChangesInterceptor`, which ignores operational fields written by the health monitor so "recently updated" stays meaningful.

**Resilient caching.** Catalogue facets and 24-hour health aggregates are cached through `IDistributedCache`. A Redis outage degrades to reading the database; it never fails a request.

**Frontend.** Zoneless Angular with signals throughout. Catalogue filters are URL state. The HTTP interceptor refreshes an expired token once and replays the request, with concurrent callers sharing a single refresh. Dialogs use the native `<dialog>` element for focus trapping and Escape handling. The initial bundle is about 82 kB transferred; every page is lazy-loaded.

---

## API reference

Full, interactive documentation is at `/swagger`. The main endpoints:

| Method | Endpoint | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| POST | `/api/auth/register` · `/login` · `/refresh` · `/logout` | Sessions (refresh token in cookie) |
| GET | `/api/auth/me` | Current user and permissions |
| GET | `/api/apis` | Search the catalogue |
| GET | `/api/apis/facets` | Filter options |
| GET · POST · PUT | `/api/apis` · `/api/apis/{id}` | Read, register, update an API |
| POST | `/api/apis/{id}/lifecycle` | Move through the lifecycle |
| POST | `/api/apis/{id}/versions` | Release a version |
| POST | `/api/apis/{id}/versions/{versionId}/promote` | Promote beta → production |
| POST · GET | `/api/apis/{id}/versions/{versionId}/openapi` | Import / download the OpenAPI document |
| GET | `/api/apis/{id}/versions/{versionId}/endpoints` | Operations in a version |
| GET | `/api/apis/{id}/versions/compare?from=&to=` | Diff two versions |
| GET · POST | `/api/apis/{id}/health` · `/health/check` | Health detail and score / probe now |
| GET | `/api/apis/{id}/analytics?range=24h\|7d\|30d` | Usage analytics |
| GET | `/api/apis/{id}/consumers` | Who consumes an API (owners) |
| POST · GET | `/api/access-requests` | Request access / list (`scope=mine\|approvals`) |
| POST | `/api/access-requests/{id}/approve` · `/reject` · `/cancel` | Decide on a request |
| GET | `/api/subscriptions` | Your credentials |
| POST | `/api/subscriptions/{id}/secret` · `/revoke` | Generate/rotate secret, revoke |
| GET | `/api/dashboard` | Overview for the signed-in user |
| GET · POST | `/api/notifications` · `/{id}/read` · `/read-all` | In-app notifications |
| GET · PUT | `/api/admin/users` · `/{id}/role` · `/{id}/status` | User administration |
| GET · POST | `/api/teams` | Teams |
| GET | `/api/audit` | Audit log search |
| WS | `/hubs/platform` | `HealthChanged` and `Notification` events |

---

## Tests and CI

```bash
cd backend && dotnet test
```

The unit tests cover the lifecycle and versioning rules, access-request and credential rules, the health score, the OpenAPI parser, password hashing and secret generation. GitHub Actions builds and tests the backend, builds the Angular app, and builds both container images on every push and pull request.

## Database and migrations

On start-up the API applies EF Core migrations if the project has any; otherwise it creates the schema directly, so a fresh clone runs with no extra steps. To move to migrations:

```bash
cd backend
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate -p src/ApiForge.Infrastructure -s src/ApiForge.API
```

From then on the app applies migrations automatically.

## Roadmap

- Gateway metrics ingestion endpoint to replace the demo traffic simulator
- OpenAPI spec storage in Azure Blob Storage and YAML import
- Integration tests with `WebApplicationFactory` and Testcontainers for SQL Server
- Azure deployment (Container Apps + Azure SQL + Azure Cache for Redis) from the CI pipeline
- Per-consumer rate limits enforced from granted permissions

